        <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl lg:text-4xl text-ink leading-tight mb-5">Are You Having a Panic Attack? Symptoms, Causes and What to Do</h1>
        <p class="text-ink-muted text-lg leading-relaxed">Racing heart, shortness of breath, fear of dying — panic attacks feel intensely real, but they are not life-threatening. Understanding what is happening is the first step.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-12 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <div class="prose-custom space-y-8">

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What is a panic attack?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">A panic attack is a sudden episode of intense fear or discomfort that typically lasts between a few minutes and half an hour. They often arise without an obvious trigger — they can occur in a shopping centre, in the car, or even during sleep.</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The brain receives a false danger signal and activates the "fight or flight" response. The physical symptoms that follow are very real, but they are not medically dangerous.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-primary/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">How common are panic attacks?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Approximately 11% of the world's population will experience at least one panic attack during their lifetime. Panic disorder affects around 2–3% of adults and is twice as common in women. (APA, 2022)</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What are the symptoms of a panic attack?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">According to DSM-5 diagnostic criteria, at least four of the following symptoms must arise abruptly and peak within minutes:</p>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-4 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <p class="text-xs font-medium text-ink mb-2">Physical</p>
                <ul class="text-xs text-ink-muted space-y-1.5">
                  <li>• Rapid or pounding heartbeat (palpitations)</li>
                  <li>• Shortness of breath or feeling of choking</li>
                  <li>• Chest pain or pressure</li>
                  <li>• Trembling or shaking</li>
                  <li>• Sweating, hot flushes</li>
                  <li>• Nausea or stomach discomfort</li>
                  <li>• Dizziness or light-headedness</li>
                  <li>• Numbness or tingling</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-4 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <p class="text-xs font-medium text-ink mb-2">Psychological</p>
                <ul class="text-xs text-ink-muted space-y-1.5">
                  <li>• Fear of losing control or "going crazy"</li>
                  <li>• Fear of dying</li>
                  <li>• Sense of unreality (derealisation)</li>
                  <li>• Feeling detached from oneself (depersonalisation)</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Difference between a panic attack and panic disorder</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">Experiencing a single panic attack does not mean you have panic disorder. Panic disorder requires:</p>
            <div class="space-y-3">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Recurrent, unexpected panic attacks.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">At least one month of persistent concern about further attacks or significant avoidance behaviour following an attack.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">The condition negatively affecting daily life, work or social relationships.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What causes panic attacks?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">There is no single cause for panic attacks. Multiple factors often work together:</p>
            <div class="space-y-3">
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Biological factors</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Hypersensitivity of the amygdala, imbalances in noradrenaline and serotonin, and genetic predisposition can all contribute to panic attacks.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Psychological factors</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">High levels of anxiety, a strong need for control, past traumatic experiences and negative thought patterns frequently accompany panic attacks.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Life stressors</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Job loss, relationship difficulties, health worries, major life changes and chronic stress can all act as triggers.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Substance and medication effects</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Excessive caffeine, nicotine withdrawal and certain medication side effects can also trigger panic attacks.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What should you do during a panic attack?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-5">The techniques below may not stop an attack immediately, but they reduce its intensity and help it pass more quickly.</p>

            <div class="space-y-4">
              <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-xs font-bold shrink-0">1</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Diaphragmatic breathing</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Breathe in through your nose for 4 counts, hold for 2 seconds, breathe out through your mouth for 6 counts. Slow breathing calms the nervous system and stops hyperventilation.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-xs font-bold shrink-0">2</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">The 5-4-3-2-1 technique</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Name 5 things you can see, 4 things you can touch, 3 things you can hear, 2 things you can smell, 1 thing you can taste. This sensory exercise anchors you to the present moment and reduces feelings of unreality.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-xs font-bold shrink-0">3</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Remind yourself "this will pass"</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Panic attacks typically peak within 10–20 minutes and then subside. Reminding yourself that you are not in danger breaks the avoidance cycle.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-xs font-bold shrink-0">4</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Do not flee the situation</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Leaving the situation brings short-term relief, but in the long run your fear of that place intensifies. Where possible, stay where you are and allow the attack to pass.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-accent/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-accent/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">When should you see a professional?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">If you are experiencing more than one panic attack a month, have started avoiding certain places out of fear of an attack, or your daily functioning has been disrupted, it is recommended that you consult a mental health professional. Panic disorder is highly treatable with Cognitive Behavioural Therapy.</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
